to-sylvia-ratnasamy-source : List Word
to-sylvia-ratnasamy-source = lines-of (cons "# Sylvia Ratnasamy"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Professor of Computer Science at UC Berkeley, with the NetSys Lab, also at Google; ACM Fellow (2025) for contributions to networks and networked systems; Grace Murray Hopper Award (2014) for the Content-Addressable Network. This page is written to you from Hyperactive Labs. Avik Jain studied EECS at Berkeley. Each claim below about his work links to the block of his paper, *hyperactivity*, Part I, that states it."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What the Content-Addressable Network established"
  (cons ""
  (cons "The field files CAN under peer-to-peer lookup: one of the four original distributed hash tables, a way to find a file without a central index. That reading is accurate and too small. What your 2001 paper established is that **the name of a thing can be computed from the thing itself**. A key hashed to a point in a d-dimensional coordinate space, a node owning the zone that contains it, each hop forwarding greedily toward that point: no registry, no agreement protocol, no one to ask. Any two machines holding the same content compute the same name independently."
  (cons ""
  (cons "That is a statement about identity, not about routing. Most of computing still names things by where they are and then spends enormous effort keeping those names consistent with what they denote. Your design showed, at Internet scale, that when the name is a function of the content, naming itself needs no agreement."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What hyperactivity takes up from it"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Its computation rules are the CCHM cubical calculus with clock-indexed guarded computation"
  (cons (cite s1-1-p4 "(§1.1)")
  (cons ", and its codebase is content-addressed: a definition is stored with its typed syntax tree and dependency references, a name is a view or alias, and a stable reference identifies a particular definition, so renaming does not rewrite the computation"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p2 "(§10.8)")
  (cons ". The paper cites Unison as its precedent there; CAN is the earlier statement of the same principle, for machines rather than for one codebase."
  (cons ""
  (cons "- **A change is a new object, never an overwrite.** A change to a definition produces a new object and determines precisely which dependent constructions may require recomputation"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p2 "(§10.8)")
  (cons "; new code is a new immutable definition, and rebinding a name publishes it in a new snapshot"
  (cons (cite s10-5-p36 "(§10.5)")
  (cons "."
  (cons "- **Reuse is governed by recorded dependencies.** A retained result carries a certificate of what it observed, including absence"
  (cons (cite s10-5-p2 "(§10.5)")
  (cons "; matching the recorded dependencies is a sufficient certificate for reusing a deterministic pure result, not a necessary one"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p6 "(§10.8)")
  (cons "."
  (cons "- **Identity needs no global namespace.** A peer can send a definition, its dependency closure and its proofs, and the recipient checks and uses them in its own context; a shared global namespace is unnecessary for definition identity"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p7 "(§10.8)")
  (cons ". Content identity establishes which definition is requested, not that a remote machine executed it, so a result is checked directly, accompanied by evidence, or accepted under a stated assumption"
  (cons (cite s10-6-p11 "(§10.6)")
  (cons "."
  (cons "- **An address is not a proof of meaning.** The digest identifies a representation under the store's collision-resistance assumption"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p3 "(§10.8)")
  (cons "; address identity, definitional equality and a specified relation between interpreted meanings are different statements, and the store records each relation with its own evidence"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p5 "(§10.8)")
  (cons "."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Composition over data, not over calls"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Your later work keeps returning to the same move. RouteBricks and the NFV line (carried into Nefeli Networks) made network functions composable software. *From Kubernetes to Knactor* argues that RPC, REST and pub/sub compositions destroy the modularity microservices were meant to provide, and proposes composition as explicit data exchanges among services."
  (cons ""
  (cons "The paper reaches the same structure. Interactions are commands in a stated signature; a handler gives each its meaning; composing a handler internalizes the interactions it covers and leaves the rest open"
  (cons (cite s7-6-p6 "(§7.6)")
  (cons ". There is no independently maintained table of which files, artifacts and deployment descriptors correspond: they are constructions connected by the operations that produce them"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p6 "(§10.8)")
  (cons ". A materialized view is maintained with a proof that its update preserves its defining equation"
  (cons (cite s10-6-p5 "(10.21)")
  (cons ", and after an edit a proof about the old program must be re-established for the new one while derived artifacts are recomputed"
  (cons (cite s10-11-p6 "(§10.11)")
  (cons "."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Invariants, checked rather than tested"
  (cons ""
  (cons "*Inductive Deductive Synthesis* (with Agarwal, Krentsel, Stoica, Zaharia, Lesani and others) starts from the fact that testing cannot establish consistency under every interleaving, and has the implementation and its proof produced together: all seven distributed key-value specifications passed where the leading coding agents passed two. *Invariant Discovery for Networked Systems* (Autogram) leaves acceptance of every invariant to solvers and statistical tests. CrossCheck validates inputs to WAN control systems before they act."
  (cons ""
  (cons "In the paper that division of labor is the language. A requirement on a function is a dependent family and the reasoning establishing it a term"
  (cons (cite s1-p25 "(1.5)")
  (cons "; tests observe selected executions, whereas that term concerns every input in its domain"
  (cons (cite s1-p26 "(§1)")
  (cons ". A specialized result is checked at its declared type before it is admitted"
  (cons (cite s6-2-p3 "(§6.2)")
  (cons ". A distributed credit invariant is proved preserved by every allowed transition with no centralized execution algorithm"
  (cons (cite s10-3-p8 "(§10.3)")
  (cons ", and a real protocol's message identities, duplication and failure rules are part of what must be stated"
  (cons (cite s10-3-p9 "(§10.3)")
  (cons "."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Digital unification"
  (cons ""
  (cons "In *Resilient Infrastructures via Digital Unification* (Communications of the ACM, with Ang Chen, Vint Cerf and others) you argue that power, water and datacenter infrastructures should share one digital layer of common abstractions, federated at their points of contact. The paper describes a layer of that kind. The same composition can be examined at the scale of a function, machine, operating system or communicating fleet"
  (cons (cite s1-3-p2 "(§1.3)")
  (cons "; a kernel and its guests form one program whose invariants are stated for the joint running program"
  (cons (cite s7-5-p4 "(§7.5)")
  (cons "; and shared computation and data cross machines and organizations without a single global execution order or one central database"
  (cons (cite s10-6-p12 "(§10.6)")
  (cons "."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Credit"
  (cons ""
  (cons "You hold full credit for every piece of your work that this system builds on: content-addressed naming without a registry, composition as explicit data exchange, and invariants admitted only by checking. Read it looking for where your work already stands inside it."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Sources"
  (cons ""
  (cons "- Sylvia Ratnasamy, Berkeley EECS faculty page, https://www2.eecs.berkeley.edu/Faculty/Homepages/ratnasamy.html"
  (cons "- Sylvia Ratnasamy, Wikipedia, https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sylvia_Ratnasamy"
  (cons "- ACM award recipient page, https://awards.acm.org/award-recipients/ratnasamy_3303665"
  (cons "- A Scalable Content-Addressable Network, https://www1.icsi.berkeley.edu/ftp/pub/techreports/2000/tr-00-010.pdf"
  (cons "- Inductive Deductive Synthesis, https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.23109"
  (cons "- Invariant Discovery for Networked Systems, https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.22944"
  (cons "- From Kubernetes to Knactor, https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.01805"
  (cons "- Resilient Infrastructures via Digital Unification, https://web.eecs.umich.edu/~chenang/papers/SIGDX-CACM.pdf"
  (cons "- Avik Jain, hyperactivity, Part I, the blocks linked above"
  nil))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))
